WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.610
Deadpool 2 was so full of Easter eggs, inside
jokes, and obscure gags that nobody could

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:05.920
possibly catch them all.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.090
Which is great, because it means that every
time you rewatch it, there's another layer

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.090
to discover.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.500
To help you on that journey, here's a look
at some of the smallest details in Deadpool

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.020
2 you might not have noticed until now.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:16.540
Up and at 'em

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.130
Deadpool 2's Super Duper Cut, released to
home audiences, restored a bunch of deleted

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:25.520
scenes to the film, but there was one truly
random joke that didn't make it into any version

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:26.520
of the film.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.090
During the sequence where Wade Wilson sets
out to meet his deceased love Vanessa in the

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.940
afterlife by offing himself over and over
again, there was originally an unexpected

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.200
homage to the beloved Pixar film Up.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:38.000
"It's a free country."

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.020
"You're right.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.720
It is a free co -- hey, I just wanna say,
I loved your work in 'Up'."

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:45.080
"Sorry?"

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.560
The movies do, oddly enough, share a lot of
thematic elements, so there's the thesis for

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:50.660
your next term paper.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:51.540
You're welcome!

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.000
The true Juggernaut

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:58.579
Unlike his appearance in 2006's X-Men: The
Last Stand, the super-strong, super-large

00:00:58.579 --> 00:01:02.210
character Juggernaut was completely computer
generated in Deadpool 2.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.750
But if you were wondering who played the role,
the end credits weren't very helpful: they

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.899
claimed that the film featured the real Juggernaut
playing himself.

00:01:08.899 --> 00:01:13.359
Turns out the truth is even weirder: the facial
motion capture was actually provided by director

00:01:13.359 --> 00:01:17.429
David Leitch, while Ryan Reynolds himself
did both the voice and the motion capture

00:01:17.429 --> 00:01:20.959
for the body, meaning in many scenes, he was
actually fighting himself.

00:01:20.959 --> 00:01:25.579
It was such a big secret that even co-star
Stefan Kapičić, who provided the motion-capture

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:27.240
for Colossus, didn't know about it.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.460
He told the Hollywood Reporter,

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:32.310
"Ryan is always there when I'm working on
Colossus, helping me with the lines and giving

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:33.040
me ideas.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.540
Then I saw Ryan getting into the CGI costume,
and I'm like, 'What's going on, man?

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:37.280
Did I miss something?'"

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.180
Rubbed the wrong way

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.140
One of the deleted scenes that ended up in
Deadpool 2's Super Duper Cut is a serene domestic

00:01:44.149 --> 00:01:48.509
sequence in the opulent Xavier mansion, in
which Wade Wilson tries to endear himself

00:01:48.509 --> 00:01:52.520
to his new roommates, following Colossus'
intervention in his mission to self-destruct.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:54.940
"Something wrong with the soap."

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:56.040
"It's fine.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:58.040
Just filled it.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.320
Give it a few more pumps."

00:02:00.609 --> 00:02:04.940
On its own, the scene seems like a non sequitur,
but it's actually setting up a truly repulsive

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.630
punchline later in the movie.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:10.180
"People think you wake up a hero, brush your
teeth a hero.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.100
Francis into a soap dispenser a hero."

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:14.640
"Bozhe moi."

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:19.960
This punchline actually appeared in the final
film without the setup, so if you were confused,

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.540
now you know the secret behind this seemingly
random moment.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:24.680
Star-studded breakfast

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.080
In a quick moment during the first fight at
the villainous Essex House, Deadpool pauses

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:32.700
after being hurled through a window to sign
an autograph for a child who never actually

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:33.700
asked for one.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.159
Grabbing a pen and a cereal box, he scribbles
across the front of the box before jumping

00:02:37.159 --> 00:02:38.159
back in the fray.

00:02:38.159 --> 00:02:41.920
You probably noticed that the box of Hero
Flakes has an image of Hugh Jackman's Wolverine

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:42.700
on the front.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.640
But in a visual gag that might have gone by
too fast to see the first time, Deadpool actually

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:48.740
signed his real name, Ryan Reynolds!

00:02:48.909 --> 00:02:52.600
We know this rivalry with Wolverine comes
from a place of hate and all, but as uninvolved

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.300
viewers, it all comes off as kind of cute.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.380
Double-dipped alternate jokes

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.240
Deadpool 2's Super Duper Cut is less of a
definitive director's cut and more of an alternate,

00:03:01.249 --> 00:03:05.430
longer version of the movie replete with different
jokes, some of which were improvised by the

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.260
actors, and some of which were scripted.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:11.140
"There's a reason I'm here, I'll know when
I know, everything usually just works out

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:11.780
for me."

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:13.020
"Like 2008 Ryan Reynolds."

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:15.320
"I don't know who that is."

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:16.240
"You're hired."

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:20.400
Reynolds and the gang apparently couldn't
decide which jokes were funniest, so instead

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.740
they just released two different versions
of the movie so you could get all the jokes.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:27.620
The opening credits are a perfect example,
with different punchlines that don't change

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.640
the story at all, but give repeat viewings
a new twist.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.200
And some of the changes, like replacing the
finger gun with a real gun, don't seem to

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.840
serve any purpose at all - which is really
the most Deadpool move of all when you think

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:38.500
about it.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:39.840
Red Lotus

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.100
As director David Leitch puts it during a
special features interview, there might be

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:48.040
Easter eggs hiding inside Deadpool 2 that
no viewer will ever discover.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:51.940
"There's so many to dig into and layers of
them, that I don't think anyone will ever

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:52.440
find them.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:53.800
I CHALLENGE you to find them all."

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:56.680
Just how deep are some of these cuts?

00:03:56.689 --> 00:04:00.590
Take a look at this business card briefly
seen during the opening action montage.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:05.239
When translated, the text is actually a reference
to an obscure X-men character named Red Lotus,

00:04:05.239 --> 00:04:08.870
who is a superpowered Australian with ties
to a Chinese crime family.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:13.889
In other words, not only do you need an encyclopedic
knowledge of X-Men lore to catch this reference,

00:04:13.889 --> 00:04:16.100
you also need to be fluent in Cantonese.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.560
So what other secrets are hiding in Deadpool
2?

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.620
Cue up Google translate and get to work.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.320
We can't wait to see what you might find.

